Rejects-invalid test: CBMC currently accepts the kind-mismatched
deduction and verifies the program.  Round-44 attempt: enforcing
[temp.deduct.type]/17 at guess_function_template_args'
build_template_args point broke the (valid) libc++ tuple kernels --
CBMC's own machinery parks deduced values with normalized kinds (e.g.
__make_integer_seq synthesizes values whose recorded type differs from
the pack's declared type), so internal value-typing must be made
consistent BEFORE the exact-match rule can be enforced.  Mind
cpp14_libcxx_tuple_ctor_kernel when retrying.

ROUND-115 attempt (three variants, all measured, all reverted):
enforcing /17 at guess_function_template_args with the deduced values'
recorded types.  (1) Full type+width comparison: rejects the invalid
kernel AND both make_integer_seq canaries stay green, but SEVEN valid
tuple/ranges tests break -- the check also caught explicitly-supplied
arguments (get<0>) where [temp.arg.nontype] permits conversion.
(2) Excluding explicitly-given parameters: five valid tests still break
-- their deduced size_t indices are RECORDED as plain int (width 32),
CBMC's internal kind normalization.  (3) Equal-width-only (sign
mismatch at same width): still three valid tests break
(tuple_get_return_type_body, ranges_pipe_invoke_drop,
tuple_class_element) -- equal-width sign-mixed recordings occur in
valid programs too.  CONCLUSION firmed up: recorded template-argument
value types carry NO reliable signal at ANY width; the prerequisite is
normalizing the recorded value types at template-argument CONVERSION
time ([temp.arg.nontype]: convert to the parameter's type when the
argument list is built), after which the /17 comparison becomes exact.
The relocated-check variant (at the template-id pack-deduction site,
using the pack parameter's symbol-table entry) does not fire at all:
template parameters have no symbol-table entries.
